#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128825 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d19e98-522f-5db2-b340-0f35ba2ece23" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "6edc9a54-82e2-5a3b-a7b5-379328a78c99" :question/math-academy-id "e-21818" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of differential equations are linear?\n\n${x_{1}^{′}(t) = \\frac{1}{2}x_{1}^{2}(t) + 5x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = \\sqrt{5}x_{1}(t) + 3\\sin (x_{2}(t))$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′}(t) = a(t)x_{1}(t) + b(t)x_{2}(t) + f(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = c(t)x_{1}(t) + d(t)x_{2}(t) + g(t)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t)$, $b(t)$, $c(t)$, $d(t)$, $f(t)$, and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n- $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the terms $x_1^2(t)$ and $\\sin(x_2(t))$.\n- System II is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a(t) = 8t, & b(t) =-1, & f(t) = 1, \\\\ c(t) = 6, & d(t) =-2, & g(t) = t^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nTherefore, only the second system is linear."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128826 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10420dd6-6f61-5db5-912b-c968d4c649c7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "62d5bd71-8137-553b-b564-80e9c25b1f18" :question/math-academy-id "e-21819" :question/problem "Consider the matrix differential equation $\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ Which of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3e^{5t} \\\\ 5e^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}(3e^{5t})^{′} \\\\ (5e^{-3t})^{′}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3e^{5t} \\\\ 5e^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}15e^{5t} \\\\ -15e^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3e^{5t} \\\\ 5e^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}15e^{5t} \\\\ -15e^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}15e^{5t} \\\\ -15e^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}(t + 5)^{′} \\\\ (t^{2} - 3)^{′}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}t + 5 \\\\ t^{2} - 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}t + 5 \\\\ t^{2} - 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix} \\mid \\ne \\begin{bmatrix}5t + 25 \\\\ -3t^{2} + 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\cdot\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $\\mathbf{x}_3(t)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}(5e^{t})^{′} \\\\ (-3e^{t})^{′}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5e^{t} \\\\ -3e^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}5e^{t} \\\\ -3e^{t}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5e^{t} \\\\ -3e^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}5e^{t} \\\\ -3e^{t}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}25e^{t} \\\\ 9e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\cdot\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128828 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dfdbdba-247c-5ddd-bb6e-5d9637140264" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Linear System of Differential Equations in Matrix Form"}] :question/id #uuid "1cd2e15e-ef47-5244-91d1-85864a62d58d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21820" :question/problem "If the following system of differential equations is written in the form $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$, then what is the matrix $A$?\n${x_{1}^{′}(t) + 3x_{1}(t) - 4x_{2}(t) = 0; x_{2}^{′}(t) - \\sqrt{2}x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t) = 0$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′}(t) = a(t)x_{1}(t) + b(t)x_{2}(t) + f(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = c(t)x_{1}(t) + d(t)x_{2}(t) + g(t)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t)$, $b(t)$, $c(t)$, $d(t)$, $f(t)$, and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n- $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\nSuch a system can always be written in the form\n$\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t) + \\mathbf f(t)$\n\nwhere\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}(t) \\\\ x_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe have the following system.\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′}(t) + 3x_{1}(t) - 4x_{2}(t) = 0; x_{2}^{′}(t) - \\sqrt{2}x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t) = 0\n$$\n\nWriting our system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t) + 4x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = \\sqrt{2}x_{1}(t) - 2x_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nSo, we can rewrite our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -2\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128829 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28939f65-9fd6-564d-a0c2-73c1038ac898" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Systems"}] :question/id #uuid "be14a013-2690-5fb8-8e2b-bbda1dca88b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-21821" :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n1. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-2x_{1}(t) + 3x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 6x_{1}(t) + x_{2}(t)$\n2. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) + t^{3}; x_{2}^{′}(t) =-x_{2}(t) + 1$\n3. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 5x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-2x_{1}(t) + 4x_{2}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′}(t) = a(t)x_{1}(t) + b(t)x_{2}(t) + f(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = c(t)x_{1}(t) + d(t)x_{2}(t) + g(t)\n$$\n\nIf $f \\equiv 0$ and $g \\equiv 0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f \\equiv 0$ and $g \\equiv 0{:}$\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a(t) =-2, & b(t) = 3, & f(t) = 0 \\\\ c(t) = 6, & d(t) = 1, & g(t) = 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- System II is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f \\not\\equiv 0$ and $g \\not\\equiv 0{:}$\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a(t) = 1, & b(t) = 0, & f(t) = t^{3} \\\\ c(t) = 0, & d(t) =-1, & g(t) = 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- System III is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f \\equiv 0$ and $g \\equiv 0{:}$\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a(t) = 5, & b(t) =-1, & f(t) = 0 \\\\ c(t) =-2, & d(t) = 4, & g(t) = 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, only the first and third systems are homogeneous."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10420dd6-6f61-5db5-912b-c968d4c649c7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d63e655-94cc-5cdc-9750-835757cf0980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126939} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ba9e86-0cfb-550b-8d05-9d7f31c6afdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x_{2}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126940} {:answer/id #uuid "d503914a-3f4a-5640-a2a4-8de616210196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x_{1}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126941} {:answer/id #uuid "026a5fec-0704-5b86-81f3-969ad5e8b0fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{3}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126942} {:answer/id #uuid "489fcca7-6e49-578f-8e8a-ab91d7d77011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1ba9e86-0cfb-550b-8d05-9d7f31c6afdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x_{2}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126940} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a1507e8-f2c9-5125-98cf-d4c244f37154" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126938}] :question/id #uuid "d0e0e3f4-a680-55b4-bb60-e56352a4c35c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339353" :question/problem "Consider the matrix differential equation $x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$. Which of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$x_{1}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}, x_{2}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{5t} \\\\ e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}, x_{3}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}t^{2} \\\\ t - 4\\end{bmatrix}$"}]}
